WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.423
In September 2025, an AI system from OpenAI solved all 12 problems of the ICPC World Finals,

00:00:07.423 --> 00:00:11.449
the top programming contest for university students.

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:13.540
That's a gold-medal result.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:20.250
Today, AI systems can also generate voices, videos, and a lot of everyday code.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.680
So why should you spend a semester learning to program?

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.248
There are 3 good reasons.

00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:32.332
And they all lead to the same place: what a computer really is,

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:34.430
and how it runs a program.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.440
Welcome to Lecture 1 of Introduction to AI Programming.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:43.464
In this first part, we'll see why programming still matters,

00:00:43.464 --> 00:00:48.118
what a program is, and how the parts of a computer work together to run one,

00:00:48.118 --> 00:00:49.710
one instruction at a time.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.762
Reason 1. AI doesn't yet handle the hardest tasks reliably.

00:00:54.762 --> 00:00:59.818
Benchmarks built from competitive programming, like LiveCodeBench Pro,

00:00:59.818 --> 00:01:05.380
which is judged by olympiad medallists, still find problems that models fail.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:11.580
And building large, complex software is much harder than solving one contest problem.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.090
This is changing fast, so check the latest results.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.878
Reason 2. You must understand code written by AI before you use it.

00:01:19.878 --> 00:01:25.840
Code that looks right can be wrong, insecure, or subtly different from what you asked for.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:31.601
Researchers even found that fine-tuning a model on one narrow task,

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:36.770
writing insecure code, made it misbehave on unrelated questions.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.920
They called this emergent misalignment.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.150
Somebody has to read and check the code.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.371
Reason 3. Computers don't understand what we say.

00:01:47.371 --> 00:01:53.297
They only follow instructions written precisely, in a programming language,

00:01:53.297 --> 00:01:56.220
such as assembly, C, Java, or Python.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.314
Think of a calculator.

00:01:58.314 --> 00:02:05.369
It can multiply, but you still learn arithmetic, so you can tell when an answer is wrong.

00:02:05.369 --> 00:02:09.570
In this course, you'll do both: use AI, and check it.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.078
Where does programming fit?

00:02:12.078 --> 00:02:15.247
Here's the big picture of computer science and AI.

00:02:15.597 --> 00:02:20.202
At the bottom is mathematics: probability and statistics,

00:02:20.202 --> 00:02:23.757
linear algebra, calculus, and discrete math.

00:02:24.107 --> 00:02:27.303
On top of it, computer science has 2 broad halves.

00:02:27.303 --> 00:02:30.500
One half makes computers better and easier to use:

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.463
computer architecture, operating systems, databases, networks,

00:02:34.463 --> 00:02:38.427
programming languages and compilers, and software engineering.

00:02:38.777 --> 00:02:42.801
The other half uses computers to solve real-world problems:

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:48.257
natural language processing, computer vision, robotics, security, and many more.

00:02:48.607 --> 00:02:51.295
Machine learning sits between the 2.

00:02:51.295 --> 00:02:55.327
And AI now runs through all of it, in both directions.

00:02:55.677 --> 00:02:59.144
AI for robotics, or AI for software engineering,

00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:02.177
uses AI to do another field's work better.

00:03:02.177 --> 00:03:06.077
And new hardware and systems for training large models

00:03:06.077 --> 00:03:08.677
use other fields to build better AI.

00:03:09.467 --> 00:03:11.057
So, what is a program?

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:16.190
A program, also called code or software, is a sequence of instructions.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:21.467
The computer takes the instructions and executes them, one after another.

00:03:21.817 --> 00:03:27.325
A program is a little piece of our intelligence, put into the computer.

00:03:27.325 --> 00:03:32.057
And like a recipe, it can be reused as many times as we like.

00:03:32.407 --> 00:03:34.371
Humans follow programs too.

00:03:34.371 --> 00:03:36.043
Here's one for a dance.

00:03:36.043 --> 00:03:38.080
Right hand around your head.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.116
Left hand around your belly.

00:03:40.116 --> 00:03:42.371
Straighten out your right hand.

00:03:42.371 --> 00:03:44.407
Left hand rests on your hip.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:50.221
Follow a long list like this, and sooner or later you'll make a mistake.

00:03:50.221 --> 00:03:55.837
Computers almost never do, whether they get 10 instructions or 10 billion.

00:03:56.187 --> 00:03:59.438
But a computer can't guess what you meant.

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:05.707
If an instruction is wrong, the computer follows the wrong instruction perfectly.

00:04:06.307 --> 00:04:11.339
So when a program misbehaves, don't ask why the computer did that.

00:04:11.339 --> 00:04:13.856
Ask which instruction told it to.

00:04:13.856 --> 00:04:18.507
Learning to program is learning to say exactly what you mean.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:24.980
A computer is hardware, the physical machine, running software, the programs.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:30.275
Almost every computer today, your laptop, your phone, a server,

00:04:30.275 --> 00:04:34.592
follows a design described by John von Neumann in 1945.

00:04:34.592 --> 00:04:37.810
It's called the von Neumann architecture.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.706
Its key idea is the stored program.

00:04:40.706 --> 00:04:44.707
The program's instructions are kept in memory, as data,

00:04:44.707 --> 00:04:47.326
right next to the data they work on.

00:04:47.326 --> 00:04:51.691
To run a different program, you load different instructions.

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:53.800
You don't rewire the machine.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.620
The theory came even earlier, from Alan Turing,

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.484
the father of theoretical computer science and artificial intelligence.

00:05:02.484 --> 00:05:05.018
He asked what can be computed at all.

00:05:05.018 --> 00:05:11.320
And he proposed the Turing test: can you tell a machine's conversation apart from a human's?

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.180
Let's look at the key components.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.768
The CPU, or central processing unit, executes your program.

00:05:18.768 --> 00:05:21.928
It's like a brain: very fast, but not smart.

00:05:21.928 --> 00:05:24.010
It only follows instructions.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.625
Main memory, or RAM, holds the running program and its data.

00:05:28.625 --> 00:05:30.402
It's fast, but temporary.

00:05:30.402 --> 00:05:32.960
Switch the power off, and it's gone.

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:36.814
Secondary storage, like a hard disk or an SSD,

00:05:36.814 --> 00:05:40.319
is slower but much larger, and it's permanent.

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:43.670
Your files and installed programs live here.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:50.425
Input devices, like the keyboard, mouse, touch screen and microphone, bring information in.

00:05:50.425 --> 00:05:55.140
Output devices, like the screen, speakers and printer, send it out.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.766
Inside the CPU there are 2 units.

00:05:57.766 --> 00:06:01.766
The control unit fetches the next instruction from memory,

00:06:01.766 --> 00:06:03.903
and works out what it asks for.

00:06:03.903 --> 00:06:08.730
The arithmetic logic unit, or ALU, has the circuits that carry it out:

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.730
adding, comparing, and so on.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:16.460
Memory comes in layers, from small and very fast to large and slow:

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:22.000
the CPU's cache, then main memory, then flash storage and hard disks.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:27.798
A hard disk reads spinning magnetic platters with a head on a moving arm.

00:06:27.798 --> 00:06:33.470
An SSD uses flash chips with no moving parts, which is why it's much faster.

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:39.471
So how does the CPU actually run a program stored in memory?

00:06:39.471 --> 00:06:43.200
Here's a tiny program for an imaginary CPU.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.587
It adds 2 and 3.

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:48.427
Memory cells 0 to 3 hold the instructions.

00:06:48.427 --> 00:06:52.084
Cells 100 to 102 hold the data: 2, 3, and 0.

00:06:52.084 --> 00:06:56.737
And the CPU has one working register, called register A.

00:06:57.087 --> 00:07:00.213
The CPU repeats one cycle, over and over.

00:07:00.213 --> 00:07:06.616
Fetch: the control unit reads the instruction at the address in the program counter,

00:07:06.616 --> 00:07:09.207
and adds 1 to the program counter.

00:07:09.557 --> 00:07:13.617
Decode: it works out what the instruction asks for.

00:07:13.617 --> 00:07:17.437
Execute: the ALU, or the memory, carries it out.

00:07:18.234 --> 00:07:21.380
Pause the video and run the cycle by hand.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:26.024
What's in register A, and in cell 102, after each instruction?

00:07:26.024 --> 00:07:29.994
And how many fetches happen before the machine stops?

00:07:30.768 --> 00:07:32.324
Now, let's run it.

00:07:32.324 --> 00:07:35.090
The program counter starts at 0.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:37.424
Fetch the load instruction.

00:07:37.424 --> 00:07:38.288
Decode it.

00:07:38.288 --> 00:07:41.659
Execute: copy cell 100 into register A.

00:07:41.659 --> 00:07:43.648
Now register A holds 2.

00:07:43.998 --> 00:07:46.566
The program counter is now 1.

00:07:46.566 --> 00:07:51.349
Fetch the add instruction: add cell 101 to register A.

00:07:51.349 --> 00:07:52.678
Now it holds 5.

00:07:53.028 --> 00:07:54.861
Address 2: store.

00:07:54.861 --> 00:07:58.095
Copy register A into cell 102.

00:07:58.095 --> 00:08:00.468
Now that cell holds 5.

00:08:00.818 --> 00:08:02.307
Address 3: halt.

00:08:02.307 --> 00:08:03.983
The machine stops.

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:06.218
That's 4 fetches in all.

00:08:06.568 --> 00:08:11.784
In Python, all of that is one line: result = 2 + 3.

00:08:11.784 --> 00:08:14.648
Print result, and you see 5.

00:08:14.998 --> 00:08:21.926
This fetch, decode, execute cycle is what it means for a computer to run your program.

00:08:21.926 --> 00:08:26.438
A modern CPU runs billions of these cycles every second.

00:08:27.235 --> 00:08:28.555
Let's sum up.

00:08:28.905 --> 00:08:31.853
A program is a sequence of instructions.

00:08:31.853 --> 00:08:36.865
The computer follows them exactly, and never guesses what you meant.

00:08:37.215 --> 00:08:41.864
A computer has a CPU, with a control unit and an ALU,

00:08:41.864 --> 00:08:47.655
plus main memory, secondary storage, and input and output devices.

00:08:48.005 --> 00:08:50.380
The program is stored in memory,

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:54.685
and the CPU runs it with the fetch, decode, execute cycle.

00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:56.725
Here's one to try.

00:08:56.725 --> 00:09:01.516
Change the little program so it computes 2 + 3 + 4,

00:09:01.516 --> 00:09:06.307
with the 4 in cell 103, and the result in cell 104.

00:09:06.307 --> 00:09:07.622
Then trace it.

00:09:07.622 --> 00:09:11.755
What's in register A after each instruction?

00:09:12.305 --> 00:09:16.042
In part 2, we'll climb from machine code up to Python,

00:09:16.042 --> 00:09:20.265
and see why everything inside a computer is stored in binary.

